-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- The Harmonic Algorithm — Jazz (:Change) Ingestion Diagnostic
-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
--
-- A top-to-bottom exploration of the new jazz corpus layer:
-- chord-symbol parser → curated tone sets → the jazz namer →
-- beatwise cadence extraction → the :Change graph on the
-- scratch container. Execute each line as you scroll.
--
-- STATUS (2026-08-25): FULL FEATURE. The :Change graph lives in
-- the core database alongside :Cadence (one DB, two labels), and
-- gen-side genJ is implemented: §7 generates and §8 plays it
-- through the normal arrange pipeline.
--
-- ── PREREQUISITES ──
-- The core database (jazz graph already ingested):
--
--     docker compose up -d neo4j
--
-- To re-ingest the jazz graph from the corpus (label-scoped,
-- classical data untouched):    stack run -- jazz
--
-- The corpus itself: ~/musicdata/Jazz-Chord-Progressions-Corpus
-- (Bunks/Weyde/Dixon/Di Giorgi, ISMIR 2023, CC BY 4.0).

-- ── 0. IMPORTS ──
-- The jazz layer is deliberately separate from Harmonic.Lib:
-- it's an ingestion module, not a performance surface (yet).

import qualified Harmonic.Rules.Import.Jazz as J

import qualified Data.Text.IO as TIO

import Harmonic.Database (connectNeo4jAt, runDb, runQuery)

import Harmonic.Rules.Types.Pitch (unPitchClass)

-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- 1. ONE SYMBOL → PITCH-CLASS CONTENT
-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- parseToken reads a leadsheet symbol exactly as notated.
-- jcTones = absolute pitch classes (0=C .. 11=B), sorted.
-- jcAnchor = the sounding fundamental: slash bass when written,
-- otherwise the chord root. Zero-form and movement are relative
-- to the anchor — the slash is honoured, never rewritten.

J.parseToken "C"

J.parseToken "Dm7"

-- Slash chord, bass NOT a chord member: G joins the set, and
-- becomes the anchor. {D F A C} ∪ {G} over G.
J.parseToken "Dm7/G"

-- Slash chord, bass IS a chord member (an inversion): the set
-- gains nothing, the anchor still moves to the bass.
J.parseToken "C/E"

-- Enharmonics fold by arithmetic — Cb is pitch class 11:
J.parseToken "Cb13#11"

-- Deep-tail alteration stack:
J.parseToken "Eb7b9#11"

-- Notated silence is a first-class token (it will BRIDGE, §4):
J.parseToken "NC"

-- Refusals are data, not crashes — unknown symbols are recorded:
J.parseToken "Hm7"

J.parseToken "Cwat"

-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- 2. THE CURATED QUALITY TABLE
-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- 122 quality spellings → canonical tone sets (semitones above
-- the root). Curated to what a player reads, not the full
-- theory stack: 13 = 1-3-b7-9-13 (no 5th/11th), dominant 11
-- omits the 3rd, alterations displace their naturals.

length J.qualityNames

-- A few lookups (Data.Map interface):
Data.Map.Strict.lookup "13" J.qualityIntervals

Data.Map.Strict.lookup "7alt" J.qualityIntervals

Data.Map.Strict.lookup "m7b5" J.qualityIntervals

-- Spelling variants collapse to one set — 122 names, 85 sets:
Data.Map.Strict.lookup "h7" J.qualityIntervals

Data.Map.Strict.size J.canonicalQuality

-- Where variants collide, the corpus-preferred spelling wins
-- (embedded census frequencies decide — e.g. the corpus writes
-- "7+" 1048 times vs "7#5" 758 times, so the node shows "7+"):
Data.Map.Strict.lookup [0,4,8,10] J.canonicalQuality

-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- 3. THE JAZZ NAMER
-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- jazzFunctionality is the pure set→name function that keeps
-- node identity and node label in lockstep. Direct table hit:

J.jazzFunctionality [0,4,7]

J.jazzFunctionality [0,3,7,10]

-- Anything else is a slash shape: some ROTATION of the set is a
-- curated quality, and the name is quality/bass-degree.
-- {0,5,9} rotated to its 5th is {0,4,7} — a major triad over
-- its fifth, i.e. G/D:
J.jazzFunctionality [0,5,9]

-- Cm/Bb: {0,2,5,9} is a rotation of {0,3,7,10} — the namer
-- discovers that Cm over Bb IS an inverted m7:
J.jazzFunctionality [0,2,5,9]

-- First-inversion dominant (G7/B):
J.jazzFunctionality [0,3,6,8]

-- Alien sets refuse rather than invent (chromatic cluster):
J.jazzFunctionality [0,1,2]

-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- 4. A REAL TUNE, BEAT BY BEAT
-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

birdText <- TIO.readFile "/Users/oscarsouth/musicdata/Jazz-Chord-Progressions-Corpus/SongDB/Songs[H-O]/LullabyOfBirdland.txt"

bird <- either (fail . show) pure (J.parseSong "LullabyOfBirdland" birdText)

J.jsTitle bird

J.jsComposer bird

J.jsTimeSig bird

length (J.jsBars bird)

-- Beat expansion: a bar's beats are shared front-loaded across
-- its chords (2 chords in 4/4 → 2+2; 3 chords → 2+1+1), and a
-- chord holding n beats repeats n times in the stream — that's
-- where pedal self-cadences come from. NC beats VANISH, so the
-- harmony either side of a silence becomes adjacent (bridge).

J.beatSlots (4,4) 2

J.beatSlots (4,4) 3

J.beatSlots (3,4) 2

length (J.beatStream bird)

-- The cadence chain: movement of the anchor + zero-form target.
-- "pedal" = sustained harmony, "asc 5" = up a fourth (the V→I /
-- ii→V motion), each named by the §3 namer:

mapM_ print (take 12 (J.songCadences bird))

-- Same shape as the classical graph key:
map J.jazzShow (take 6 (J.songCadences bird))

-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- 5. HEAR IT (d01, one beat per cycle)
-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- The tune's beat stream as raw tone sets — not generation,
-- just the parsed corpus data made audible. 1 cycle = 1 beat.

birdNotes = map (map ((+ 48) . unPitchClass) . J.jcTones) (J.beatStream bird)

d01 $ n (cat (map (stack . map (pure . fromIntegral)) birdNotes)) # ch 01

hush

-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- 6. THE :Change GRAPH (core database probes)
-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- Same database, same NEXT edges, disjoint label — the classical
-- :Cadence graph is untouched by jazz ingest (verified by count +
-- digest gates at promotion).

conn <- connectNeo4jAt "http://localhost:7474"

runDb conn (runQuery "MATCH (n:Change) RETURN count(n) AS nodes")

runDb conn (runQuery "MATCH (:Change)-[r:NEXT]->(:Change) RETURN count(r) AS edges, round(sum(r.confidence)) AS mass")

-- The graph speaks jazz: the heaviest non-self edges are the
-- ii-V-I, by an order of magnitude.
runDb conn (runQuery "MATCH (a:Change)-[r:NEXT]->(b:Change) WHERE a.show <> b.show RETURN a.show, b.show, r.confidence ORDER BY r.confidence DESC LIMIT 8")

-- Out of any node, the walk's future candidates (this is what
-- gen-side genJ will rank and sample):
runDb conn (runQuery "MATCH (c:Change {show: '( pedal -> m7 )'})-[r:NEXT]->(n:Change) RETURN n.show, r.confidence ORDER BY r.confidence DESC LIMIT 12")

-- Per-composer weights ride every edge (sparse JSON, summed
-- into confidence — identical machinery to the classical graph):
runDb conn (runQuery "MATCH (a:Change {show: '( pedal -> m7 )'})-[r:NEXT]->(b:Change {show: '( asc 5 -> 7 )'}) RETURN r.confidence, left(r.weights, 200)")

-- One composer's voice, isolated (Monk's node vocabulary):
runDb conn (runQuery "MATCH (a:Change)-[r:NEXT]->(b:Change) WHERE r.weights CONTAINS 'theloniousmonk' RETURN count(r) AS monkEdges")

-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- 7. GENERATION: genJ
-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- The jazz walk. Same modifier chain as gen/genE/genP; the R
-- constraint IS the graph (no consonance fallback — measured:
-- ~219-candidate typical pools, 0 dead ends, 99.6% reachability).
-- Chords come out at corpus arity (3-6 tones).
--
-- SEEK SEMANTICS (one spec, both corpora):
--   seek "*"                  whole-corpus frequency walk
--   seek "monk:60 coltrane:40"  jazz-composer blend (matched by
--                             substring against jazz keys — weight
--                             splits across "theloniousmonk" etc.)
--   seek "debussy"            no jazz names -> jazz walk on "*",
--                             classical STEER: each step, the
--                             current chord's most consonant
--                             embedded triad asks the classical
--                             graph "what would Debussy do?", and
--                             jazz candidates containing a
--                             recommended triad (same movement)
--                             get boosted.
--   seek "monk debussy"       blend AND steer together.
--   seek "none"               refused — no offline jazz mode.

-- leadJ parses a leadsheet symbol into a jazz-named cue. (Do not use
-- initCadenceState here: it truncates anything past three pitch classes,
-- so "Cm7" would silently become a Cm triad.)
jstart <- leadJ "Cm7"

jstart

sj <- seek "mingus" $ cue jstart $ len 8 $ entropy 0.3 $ genJ

-- Standard trace: per-step walk lines (node -> node, pool, pick):
sj2 <- seek "theloniousmonk:60 johncoltrane:40" $ cue jstart $ len 8 $ entropy 0.5 $ genJ'

-- Verbose: adds seek resolution + steer match counts:
sj3 <- seek "debussy" $ cue jstart $ len 8 $ entropy 0.3 $ genJ''

-- Entropy dial behaves identically to the classical families:
-- 0 hugs the corpus's strongest continuations (lots of ii-V-I),
-- 1 wanders around rank 10, 2+ gets genuinely outside.
sj4 <- seek "*" $ cue jstart $ len 8 $ entropy 1.2 $ genJ

-- Single-composer viability (measured subgraphs — tunes/edges/
-- weighted branching): rodgers 66/813/39, arlen 51/1086/43,
-- monk 59/770/31, porter 42/713/35, coltrane 45/467/23,
-- parker 45/309/22. Top-dozen composers walk independently;
-- thinner names want blends. A blend that dead-ends mid-walk
-- widens to "*" for that step and says so.

-- ── STEER DIAL ──
-- Classical steering strength (default 3.0; 0 = off). At 8 a
-- Debussy steer audibly drags the walk into maj7 colour:

sj5 <- seek "debussy" $ steer 8 $ cue jstart $ len 8 $ entropy 0.3 $ genJ'

-- ── TONAL CONSTRAINTS ──
-- The same R filter as every family, applied to jazz candidates
-- (absolute tones from the anchor). Key of C pins the walk to
-- diatonic corpus vocabulary — Am7, Dm7, G7sus4, CM7, C/B...
-- A step the filter empties is relaxed with a notice (there is
-- deliberately no fallback pool to fill from).

ctxC = harmonicContext "*" "0#" "*"

sj6 <- seek "*" $ cue jstart $ tonal ctxC $ len 8 $ entropy 0.4 $ genJ'

-- ── REGENERATE IN PLACE ──
-- genJ output is stamped as jazz-family: genFrom regenerates a
-- range jazz-natively (bars outside the range byte-identical,
-- seam movement metadata fixed by the standard splice).

sj7 <- seek "*" $ entropy 0.6 $ genFrom sj 3 4

-- ── MULTI-ATTEMPT ──
-- attempt N K ranks candidates against the Change graph itself
-- (jazz cadence-favourability + the shared rm/vl/mv components);
-- genJ'' prints the scoreboard:

sj8 <- seek "*" $ attempt 3 8 $ cue jstart $ len 8 $ entropy 0.4 $ genJ''

-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- 8. PLAY IT (the normal performance pipeline, unchanged)
-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- genJ output is an ordinary ProgressionContext: voicings,
-- kinetics, arrange, subKick all work as with gen/genE/genP.

flow (triadLayer sj)

grid (triadLayer sj)

pcj = fromProgression (triadLayer sj)

kj = iK 120 [at 0 1 1 pcj] (rep pcj 1)

d01 $ arrange (0,1) kj (-9,9) T flow id ["[0 1 2 3]/4"] # ch 01

hush

-- Walking bass under it (channel 8). This is the real thing, not
-- a root-per-bar arrange: lineHarmony sees pcj's FJazz family and
-- routes it through walkLineJ, so each bar walks its chord
-- symbol's bass vocabulary — restored fifths on the 13ths, the #5
-- on altered dominants, colour tones kept off strong beats.
d08 $ lineHarmony 0.9 kj root ["[1 2 3 4]/4"] # ch 08 # legato 0.95

hush
